  "narrative": {
    "intro": "This ranking shows federal contract obligations for FY2026 to date. Total federal contract obligations are $547B. The Department of Defense obligated $278B. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $68B. The Department of Homeland Security obligated $57.6B. The Department of Energy obligated $48.3B. The General Services Administration obligated $20.1B. The Department of Health and Human Services obligated $15B. The National Aeronautics and Space Administration obligated $10.7B. The Department of Transportation obligated $9.4B. The Department of Agriculture obligated $7B. The Department of Justice obligated $6.9B. These agency-level obligations are reported as contract obligations and represent fiscal year to date totals. Readers should treat current-year totals as partial because they reflect FY2026 to date rather than a full fiscal year.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agency leads federal contract spending?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $278B. The Department of Defense stands above other agencies in absolute contract obligations for FY2026 to date. The Department of Veterans Affairs is the second-largest obligator at $68B, followed by the Department of Homeland Security at $57.6B. The ranking lists agencies by contract obligations so agencies appear here by dollar totals."
      },
      {
        "heading": "How does contract spending distribute across the top agencies?",
        "body": "The top ten agencies together appear within the $547B total federal obligations for FY2026 to date. The Department of Energy obligated $48.3B, the General Services Administration obligated $20.1B, and the Department of Health and Human Services obligated $15B. Smaller top-ten obligations include the National Aeronautics and Space Administration at $10.7B, the Department of Transportation at $9.4B, the Department of Agriculture at $7B, and the Department of Justice at $6.9B."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
